This is a Next.js + SQLite membership dashboard for a JCI chapter.
Read README.md before any change. It is the spec of record: how the logic
works, how permissions are enforced, and where this build made a judgement call.

Architecture — do not change without discussing it first:
- Python (src/, scripts/) owns ALL logic: derived fields, health score,
  alert rules, permissions. It runs at build time and emits five
  pre-filtered JSON payloads, one per demo persona, to web/data/.
- Next.js (web/) only renders those payloads, in SERVER components.
- There is no API route for member data, no runtime database, no ORM,
  no auth library. Do not add one.

Rules:
- Permission filtering happens in Python, in src/permissions.py, before the
  JSON is written. Never filter in a React component, and never ship a field
  to the client that the role may not see. Hidden field groups are DROPPED,
  never hidden with CSS.
- WRITES go through src/mutations.py and scripts/mutate.py, never straight to
  SQLite from TypeScript. That module owns which permission level may change
  which field group, what a valid value is, and which changes owe the journey
  an event. web/lib/mutations.ts only shells out to it.
- A write MUST be followed by scripts/export_json.py. The payloads are the
  permission boundary, so a database that has moved on from them is one whose
  access rules are stale. Then call clearPayloadCache() — lib/data.ts keeps
  parsed payloads in a module-level Map that revalidatePath() does not reach.
- Only STORED columns are writable. Derived fields (age, health score, fee
  status, requirement status) are recomputed on every read; a write to one is
  refused by name in mutations.py rather than accepted and then overwritten.
- FULL in permissions.MATRIX is a READ level for every tier except the one
  that owns the member database. identity is FULL for all four tiers, so
  reading FULL as a write right would let a Chairman rewrite the chapter's
  identity records and let an HS type "P" into their own board post and
  become President + MA. EDIT is the write level; FULL adds write only for
  President + MA.
- Adding, editing and erasing a member re-checks the tier in the server
  action AND again in Python. A page not drawing a button is not an access
  control — a server action is a POST endpoint.
- Field-group permissions must follow the data into EVERY table, not just
  members. status_events.reason once leaked a removal reason that the
  governance group was correctly hiding. Check new tables against the matrix.
- web/lib/data.ts imports "server-only". Never remove that, and never import
  it from a client component.
- Derived fields (age, deadlines, requirement status, project role, health
  score, WhatsApp group) are computed in src/derived.py or src/health.py.
  Never store them in the database.
- Colours come from web/lib/theme.ts only. Never write a hex literal in a
  page or component. Chart series use the PRD 13.2 values, not the raw brand
  hexes — they were validated for colour-vision separation.
- Never use colour alone: every health band and alert carries an icon and a
  text label too.
- Every table needs an empty state. Blank panels make a demo look broken.
- Keep functions under 40 lines. No class hierarchies. No premature
  abstraction.
- After changing anything in src/ or scripts/: re-run
  `python scripts/export_json.py && python scripts/verify.py`.
  verify.py must exit 0. It checks the seven alert counts against the PRD.
- Vocabulary, in the UI and in comments: a member holds POSTS (President,
  Honorary Secretary), which earn a PERMISSION LEVEL (one of four), which
  grants a FIELD ACCESS level per group (full / edit / read / masked /
  hidden). Do not write "role" for a permission or "tier" in user-facing
  copy. Post codes are rendered through web/lib/posts.ts, never printed raw.
- Do not add features that are not in the PRD. If the PRD is silent, ask.
